            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{L-1}
            are hidden layers. In particular, a real-valued 1-hidden layer neural network with computes:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = b' + \sum_{i=1}^n a_i g(\langle W_i, x\rangle + b)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = (a_1, \dots, a_n)
            is the output weight,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b'
            the output bias,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_i
            the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^{th}
            row of the hidden weight matrix, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            the hidden bias. Here, the hidden layer is
            = succeed "-dimensional." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#large_network_theory Large network theory
          %p
            For simplicity, consider a fully connected network with one layer. In terms of the notation above, consider
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℝ
