            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            to sample from the quartic actions (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4
            \-theory.) Conversely, Feynman diagram tools have been used to study the expected properties of a random-initialized network.
          %h2#learning_theory Learning theory
          %p
            A neural network architecture specifies parameters and one then learns a prescription
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\theta
            on how to get close to many given target positions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i \mapsto \theta_{i+1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_x
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mi i
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{\theta_i} \mapsto f_{\theta_{i+1}}
          %p
            Improving the amount of computational shortcuts taken in implementing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_\theta
            is what
            %em pytorch
            and
            %em tensorflow
            is largely about. There is a neural tangent library on the google github. Notable, also from google, there are some recent improvements related to baking the statistics of the collection of data (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            ) into the learning process (self-attention, transformers).
          %h3#neural_tangent_kernel_theory Neural tangent kernel theory
          %p
            As is common in stochastic control, one may look at the step sizes in a very fine limit, i.e. pass to calculus proper. The algorithm is then expressed as the vector equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi θ
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta'(t) = -\nabla_\theta\Phi
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi z
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi = \sum_z C(f_{\theta(t)}(z), y_z)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            ranges over the available learning data. The mathematical derivations end up having a similar flavor as the study of the motion of a particle. Here the evolution of all components of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            are governed by a gradient of a potential that depends on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            through the costs of some property,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\theta
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi t
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_t f_\theta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi t
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_t f_\theta
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Θ
                      %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi θ
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi θ
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta_\theta(x,z) = \nabla_\theta f(x)\cdot \nabla_\theta f(z)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Θ
                    %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta_\theta
            quantifies a similarity reminiscent of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+method"} kernel method
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\theta
            (w.r.t. a change of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            resp.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %strong neural tangent kernel theory
            (NTK theory). Formal relations between different learning approaches (neural networks and kernel machines in particular) become simpler here. Depending on the loss function, the structure may remain Gaussian throughout the learning. For a shallow network of finite size,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Θ
                    %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta_\theta
            may still give a quantitative idea on how that (finite) network will change upon tuning of its parameters. Some work was done to try to establish where NKT results start to significantly fail for practical networks. Note: Infinite networks (where weights barely need to be tuned to learn) also don’t encode abstracted features.
          %h2#relation_to_differential_equations_and_dynamical_systems Relation to differential equations and dynamical systems
          %p
            A relation between deep neural networks,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equations"} differential equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamical+systems"} dynamical systems
            was proposed in (
            %a{:href => "#CMHRBH17"}> CMHRBH17
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LZLD17"} LZLD17
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Weinan17"} Weinan17
          %p
            Victor Lopez-Pastor and Florian Marquardt proposed that certain
            %span.newWikiWord
              time-reversible
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/time-reversible"} ?
            %span.newWikiWord
              Hamiltonian systems
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Hamiltonian+systems"} ?
            exhibit self-learning behaviour and a physical version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/backpropagation"} backpropagation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithm"} algorithm
          %h2#RelationToRenormalizationGroupFlow Relation to renormalization group flow
          %p
            A relation between deep neural networks (DNNs) based on Restricted Boltzmann Machines (RBMs) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            was proposed in (
            %a{:href => "#MS14"}> MS14
            ).
